    {
      "filename": "February-March 2011 FrontLines Photo Contest Second Place (5856251119).jpg",
      "title": "File:February-March 2011 FrontLines Photo Contest Second Place (5856251119).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/February-March_2011_FrontLines_Photo_Contest_Second_Place_%285856251119%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:February-March_2011_FrontLines_Photo_Contest_Second_Place_(5856251119).jpg",
      "width": 2000,
      "height": 1333,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "USAID U.S. Agency for International Development",
      "description": "In Swaziland, just before sunset, a young girl tests out a new seesaw on a playground built by the Elizabeth Glaser Pediatric AIDS Foundation at the Mkhulamini Clinic in Swaziland. Volunteers for the foundation built the playground and a covered waiting area for patients and their children attending the clinic for HIV testing, counseling, and care and treatment. This year, it will launch the USAID-funded, five-year program, Eliminating Pediatric AIDS in Swaziland, to expand services to prevent mother-to-child transmission of HIV. Swaziland is home to the world’s most severe HIV/AIDS epidemic. Women and children are particularly affected, with 42 percent of pregnant women who receive antenatal care testing HIV-positive. July 2008.\n\nPhoto credit: Jon Hrusa, Elizabeth Glaser Pediatric AIDS Foundation.",
      "dateCreated": "Taken on 4 July 2008, 15:48",
      "categories": [
        "Eswatini",
        "Eswatini photographs taken on 2008-07-04",
        "Files uploaded by Reguyla",
        "Flickr images reviewed by FlickreviewR",
        "PD USAID",
        "Seesaws",
        "USAID IMAGES"
      ],
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "eswatini-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "keq3"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Swaziland - umhlanga or reed dance (6404664597).jpg",
      "title": "File:Swaziland - umhlanga or reed dance (6404664597).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a3/Swaziland_-_umhlanga_or_reed_dance_%286404664597%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swaziland_-_umhlanga_or_reed_dance_(6404664597).jpg",
      "width": 3168,
      "height": 4752,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "Retlaw Snellac Photography from Belgium",
      "description": "Swaziland - Umhlanga or Reed Dance.\nI had the wonderful opportunity to see the Reed Dance or \"Umhlanga\" which takes place in \n\nthe spring every year. Young childless unmarried maidens from all over the Kingdom cut reeds with long knives and then gather and present them to the Queen Mother and dance, colourfully dressed and bare breasted. The ceremony lasts eight days with the King only attending the last day's ceremony. The girls wear coloured tassels and woollen streamers denoting whether they are betrothed or not. The Princesses wear red feathers in their hair. The Swazis are a proud, wonderfully friendly nation and Swaziland is a beautiful country to visit.",
      "dateCreated": "2010-08-01 00:00",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "eswatini-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "keq3"
    },
